@Misc
$# Misc#

   Dialog API additionaly export #sparse#-function to parse
  text. Though, it's not a "regexp" but rather powerful tool
  for many cases. 

   #sparse# parse source and save processed result in output buffer.
   #parseNum# return the number of terms in buffer.
   #parseGetStr# return term from buffer with given number.
   #parseGet# - work as previous function but memory for result string
                is allocated by function. Free it manualy later.

  ~int sparse(char *source, char *format, char * dest, int len)~@sparse
  ~int parseNum(char *str)~@parseNum
  ~char *parseGetStr(char *str, char *dst, int num)~@parseGetStr
  ~char *parseGet(char *str, int num)~@parseGet

  ~Dialog API~@API
  ~Back~@Contents

@parseGet
$# parseGet#

  #char *parseGet(char *str, int num)#
   #str# - string-output of #sparse#-function.
   #num# - item number (starting from 0).

   Return pointer to result string. Memory for result is allocated
   on request. Free memory manualy after using.

  ~Dialog API~@API
  ~Back~@Contents

@parseGetStr
$# parseGetStr#

  #char *parseGetStr(char *str, char *dst, int num)#
   #str# - string-output of #sparse#-function.
   #dst# - result string.
   #num# - item number (starting from 0).

  Fill dst with result and return pointer to dst.

  ~Dialog API~@API
  ~Back~@Contents

@parseNum
$# parseNum#

  #int parseNum(char *str)#
   #str# - string-output of #sparse#-function.
   Return number of item in str.

  ~Dialog API~@API
  ~Back~@Contents
@sparse
$# sparse#

  #int sparse(char *source, char *format, char * dest, int len)#
   #source# - string to parse
   #format# - parse format
   #dest  # - buffer to output result
   #len   # - buffer size
  This function is very similar to #sscanf#.
  Return the number of treated terms (specifications and
  non-spaces sequences).

  #format# - consists of white-spaces, non-spaces and format
            specifications .
             When a space is encountered - all consecutive
            spaces in the source will be skiped.
             Non-spaces (exept '%') - will be matched with
            the source symbols.
            Non successful match will led to break function.
             Format specifications are used to read and
            save specific terms from the source.
            Specification starts from '%' :
            #% [+|*] [size] [case] type [~~default~~]#
            #[+|*] #  - #+# - "append" readed term with
                           next term
                      #*# - treate term but not save it
            #[size]#  - maximum length of term
            #[case]#  - symbols case treatment
                        #l# - "lowercase" before match
                        #u# - "uppercase" before match
                        #n# - non-case matching
            #[~~default~~]# - if term is not matched
                          symbols beetwen ~~~~ used
                          as a result.
                          Example: #S:" asdfg" - F:" %d~~0~~"#
                          that is: read number if not result
                          is #"0"#.
            #type#   - term type.
                       #c#        - read symbol
                       #t#        - read all symbols up to the
                                    end of the source
                       #s#        - read string
                       #d#        - read number
                       #[pattern]# - read symbols if they present
                                    in a pattern.
                                    If pattern start from
                                    #"^"# - that symbols are readed
                                    that not present in the pattern.
                       #{}#       - all symbols between #{}#
                                    are saved as a result.
                       #[S][q](s1[-]s2)# - read symbols that are
                                    enclosed between s1 and s2.
                                    If #"-"# exist nested
                                    matching is used.
                                    #q# - save enclosing symbols too.
                                    #S# - read string if no enclosing
                                    symbols are exist.
                                    Example:
                                      #S:" (a+(b-c))"-F:" %S((-))"#
                                    that is: read item that is
                                    enclosed by nested brackets.
                                    If no brackets read string. 
                                    Result: "a+(b-c)".
                                    if case:
                                     #S:" a+(b -c))"-F:" %S((-))"#
                                    Result: "a+(b".
   Exapmle:
  Dialog item description is parsed as follow:
  #"%us %s %d %d %d %d %uc %Sq(\"\") %uS(()) %uc %u(%%)~~TXT~~ "#
  #"%u(%%)~~HKCU~~ %S(\"\")~~NULL~~ %S(\"\")~~NULL~~"#

  You may use format to use case conversation before matching
  non-space symbols.
  Format: #%{l|u|n}' '# - space after format is important
  to differ from format specification.
  Example: S:" AsD" - F:" %l asd"
  Result : "lowercase" source before matching. Matching successful.

   There is another "pseudo-specification" :#%[size]f#.
  Example : #%2f#. Its role is to flush output buffer if any error
  in mathing occured. "Size" - give the threshold, if count of
  treated terms is less then this size buffer will be flushed.

  ~Dialog API~@API
  ~Back~@Contents
@Menu
$# General menu#
  You have 4 main commands for it:

  #Date-n-Time (Auto)#      This command use default date
                           format and autodetect destination.
                           In editor      - result is inserted
                                            at current line
                                            and position.
                           In file panel  - insert in command
                                            line
                           In other cases - result is copied
                                            to clipboard.
                           

  #Date-n-Time (Menu)#      Here you may select needed format
                           from menu and edit formats as you wish.
                            In menu :
                           Ins - for new format,
                           F4  - for editing,
                           Del - for deleting,
                           Enter, 0-9 - to select item.

  #Date-n-Time (Indicator)# This command write current date at the
                           right upper corner (near time indicator)
                           for a short time.

  #Calendar#                 Calendar with current month will be
                           showed. You may change month, year
                           and insert selected date to editor,
                           command line or clipboard.

^#Menu#

  You may select needed item or edit it.
  To edit:
   #Ins# - Create new item.
   #F4 # - If item exist Edit it, in other case Create new.
   #Del# - Delete item, no message will be shown.

  To select:
   #Enter# - select current item.
   #0-9  # - select item with such number.


  ~Main dialog description~@MainDialog

  ~Back~@Contents

